{"entries":{"chapter-26-study-legal-issues|Chapter 26 Study: Legal Issues":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|This chapter describes U.S. law: federal, state and local rules overlap, and details vary by jurisdiction (p361).":["u3"],"copy|Professional licensure generally operates at state level; payment rules and institutional regulation add obligations (p361–362).":["u4"],"copy|A malpractice claim requires duty, breach, injury and a causal connection; an adverse outcome alone does not establish negligence (p362).":["u5"],"copy|The standard is reasonable care under the circumstances; records, expert evidence and professional guidance can inform it (p362–364).":["u6"],"copy|Valid medical decisions require voluntariness, adequate information and decision-making capacity (p364).":["u7"],"copy|Explain the intervention, risks, benefits, alternatives and refusal; consent is a process, not merely a signed form (p364–365).":["u8"],"copy|Emergency treatment may proceed under implied consent when the patient cannot decide and delay threatens life or function (p365).":["u9"],"copy|Capacity is functional and decision-specific, may fluctuate, and may improve with support or a better setting (p365–366).":["u10"],"copy|Incapacity does not remove the consent obligation: work with an appropriate surrogate (p365).":["u11"],"copy|A durable health-care power of attorney names an agent; a living will records treatment instructions (p366).":["u12"],"copy|An immediate proxy appointment differs from one activated by a specified later event; explain transfer of authority to the patient (p366).":["u13"],"copy|Written directives make wishes easier to establish; revisit plans as circumstances change (p366).":["u14"],"copy|POLST documents medical orders for a seriously ill patient's current treatment preferences; its legal operation depends on the applicable setting (p366–367).":["u15"],"copy|Protect sensitive information obtained through care; both state and federal law impose duties (p367–368).":["u16"],"copy|Exceptions include patient permission and certain legally required reports or serious threats; disclosure duties depend on the circumstances and jurisdiction (p367).":["u17"],"copy|HIPAA requirements apply to covered entities and include privacy/security protections; confidentiality has specific exceptions, including treatment, payment and health-care operations (p368).":["u18"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u19"],"copy|CHAPTER 26s / HAZZARD":["u20"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 26; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u21"],"copy|U.S. legal framework":["u22"],"copy|Valid decisions & capacity":["u23"],"copy|Plan before incapacity":["u24"],"copy|Confidentiality":["u25"],"copy|No past-exam questions are currently linked to this chapter.":["u26"],"copy|Six past questions link to this chapter through Israeli statutes and Ministry instructions, with no direct Hazzard citation.":["u26"],"copy|Implied consent comes from a patient’s behavior demonstrating willingness to undergo the intervention. Emergency treatment of an incapacitated patient without an available surrogate is a separate exception (pp364–365).":["u9"],"copy|POLST documents medical orders for a seriously ill patient's current treatment preferences and travels with the patient across care settings; its legal operation depends on the applicable setting (p366–367).":["u15"],"copy|On p365, implied consent is expressed by a patient’s actions, such as voluntarily submitting to a specific routine intervention.":["u176"],"copy|CHAPTER 26 / HAZZARD":["u20"]},"maxId":176,"sequenceOffsets":[[177,-150],[301,-146],[302,-151],[306,-150]]}
